WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.050 align:start position:0%
 
foreign  is a game designed to frighten you  it depicts a biomechanical nightmare a  fever dream where everything you see is  a shocking amalgamation of metal and  meat yet under the foreboding surface  the world of scorn is a masterpiece of  Haunting and poignant World building  bringing to life a fascinating ecosystem  of Flesh and Decay while the game is  ambiguous by Design lacking any text or  dialogue a surprising number of answers  are waiting for those willing to look  for them so for this entry into the  archive will embark on an expedition  across this disturbing realm comparing  environmental details with Clues from  the art book to uncover the shocking  Secrets behind the Terrors on screen  now let's awaken to the nightmare of  scorn  we'll begin with a birth  the humanoid creatures of the scoring  Universe aren't born from a nurturing  parent but burst fully grown from an  unnatural wall and then plummet to the  ground where many of their stories end  as abruptly as they began the humanoid  protagonists you play as for most of the  game comes into the world from this  unfeeling system  after hitting the ground it's hard not  to stare up at the rows and rows of  ruptured cocoons and think about what it  would be like if this was the first  thing you ever saw this immense  structure referred to in the art book as  the Genesis wall is one of the most  striking Concepts in the world of scorn  dried husks lie beside the embankment  humanoids who perished through no fault  of their own there's a profound  unfairness at play that Echoes the  arbitrary nature of life one of the  game's core themes like many things in  Scorn the wall seems to be both  artificial and biological as there are  places where the outer layer Falls away  to reveal a mass of living organs  while the design of the Genesis wall was  conceived through stunning concept art  where the Wall came from is one question  the game's developer ebb software  declines to answer  the wall simply is a fact is immutable  as existence itself  beyond the wall shadow a desolate  Wasteland greets newborn humanoids as  they take their first steps cold the  field this is a kingdom of dust in ruin  a Barren land is seemingly stripped of  all natural resources through the fog  the forms of distant buildings reveal  themselves wind-worn structures silently  decomposing like the carcasses of  gargantuan animals  these haunting Vistas conjure the  imagery of decaying ships present in  sci-fi horror films like alien in  Prometheus the late Swiss artist H.R  Giger who worked on the biomechanical  designs for the original alien film was  a notable influence on the world of  scorn  some of the decaying ruins form vaguely  organic archways which serve as a subtle  way to pull players onwards one of these  thresholds leads to a cemetery of  machines where industrial vents Mark the  landscape like solemn gravestones at a  glance this industrial Mausoleum seems  as Barren as the rest of the field yet  defying All Odds a strange form of life  takes shelter among the headstones  seemingly originating all the way back  at the Genesis wall red tendrils spread  across the waists like an Insidious  infection and like hermit crabs seeking  a shell some masses of tentacles have  come to make their home within the vents  these parasitic growths are all that can  survive this unforgiving climate  to the side of the cemetery the largest  building in the region is a monolithic  structure called the assembly the last  Bastion of shelter in a ruined land many  forms of life make their home within  entering the assembly is like exploring  the body of a long dead giant the  hallways and cavernous Chambers call to  mind fossilized organs with the ribbed  pattern on the walls unmistakably  organic this biomechanical interior  design once again evokes the spirit of  giger's work in the alien franchise  where even buildings take on the  qualities of living things while the  bodily systems of the assembly have long  laid dormant you can guide the playable  humanoid to reawaken the decrepit  technology but be aware interacting with  technology and Scorn is never Pleasant  buttons and switches are fleshy and  bizarre to the point or something as  simple as calling an elevator is vaguely  unsettling and the more you explore and  interact with the interior the more the  original purpose of the assembly becomes  apparent the entire labyrinthine  structure functions as a kind of factory  enormous retro industrial cranes are  positioned to move cargo their  articulated hooks still sharp enough to  be menacing spine like Railways snake  through the assembly implying transport  carts once rattled through this area an  elevated control panel provides access  to a manipulatory claw arm like the  world's largest and most disturbing  arcade machine yet a question remains  just what was this Factory processing  the answer is the last available  resource in the world of scorn living  things  at the base of this world's  biomechanical ecosystem or the tragic  mold men these pitiable life forms are  the biomass the assembly is designed to  produce and are grown within hard shells  heaps of mold men husks have been swept  away to the corners of the assembly with  the size of these piles giving us some  glimpse of the scale of this factories  in humanity when it was in full  production the visuals of the mold men  are evocative of the work of Polish  artists the gisla beksinski a painter  who explored themes of entropy and Decay  beksinski's art was a key influence on  the look of the game with its emphasis  on somber visions of dried calcified  husks the imagery of a mold man sitting  in a transport chair with their limbs  extending out from the shell also calls  to mind the famous Giger portrait of the  space jockey this ambiguous visual of a  being seemingly fused with a chair which  was brought to life in the alien  franchise was a key influence in scorn's  development according to concept artist  Philip akovic the team found inspiration  not just in how the design merged  technology and living tissue but also in  its mysterious nature in the original  alien film next to nothing is revealed  about the strange being and that  uncertainty encourages viewers to  reflect on the visual likewise much  about the mold men and their connection  to the assembly's creators is left an  intriguing mystery  deeper within the assembly monolithic  towers of tissue grow in temperature  controlled greenhouse-like Chambers like  so many things in Scorn the purpose of  these looming structures is left  ambiguous with the mystery gnawing at  the back of the player's mind as they  walk in their Shadow a melting pattern  of what appears to be calcified bone  encircles the tops of these columns  suggesting they produce organic material  used elsewhere in the factory  the Legion of flying drones tends to the  tissue Towers every need hydrating the  chamber like devoted gardeners each  drone can release a steady spray of  chemical Mist which is noxious to  breathe in but seemingly critical for  the growth of the columns light from the  drones glowing bulbous shells cuts  through the Gloom bathing the chamber in  an eerie glow like dutiful worker bees  the drones stay fixed on their role in  this biomechanical ecosystem never  reacting to anything that passes by  foreign  epicenter of the greenhouse the tissue  towers are overshadowed by a Sinister  Central tree this massive organism is  far larger than anything else in the  region and appears to be the parent of  the unsettling Forest that grows around  it a unique type of carrier drone tends  to this Goliath taking vials of the  fertilizing chemical and plugging them  directly into the tree  yet these chemicals aren't the only fuel  source in the assembly even deeper  within the facility a strange structure  with branching pods seems to supply  energy activating this device will cause  the pods to open in pedal-like  Arrangements revealing humanoids kept in  suspended animation inside these  humanoids are closer to the player's  character than the mold men in cold mind  the living batteries seen in the  dystopian future of films like The  Matrix  the more you explore the lower levels of  the assembly the more the Interiors  become increasingly organ-like  extendable Bridges link up sections like  valves in a heart tubes twist through  the region like veins and some literally  seem to be veins a corrosive substance  analogous to stomach acid clears up  obstructions interacting with these  systems requires the continued use of  Technology scorn does include some  traditional video game items like keys  and weapons but filtered through a  biomechanical lens they become  unrecognizable a device that opens doors  looks like a fleshy worm a key you pick  up elsewhere in the game resembles  ringed vertebrae making it a literal  skeleton key to reload your main weapon  you approach this nightmarish machine  which loads bug-like ammunition into a  living starfish Satchel thematically  these visually disquieting inner actions  seem to examine the relationship between  humanity and Technology exploring the  intersection of the organic and  inorganic is a theme that's  unsurprisingly Central to the  biomechanical art movement returning  once again to Giger while it's easy to  just focus on the cool looking surface  imagery much of Geiger's work is meant  to interrogate whether humans are losing  themselves to technology it's a question  that in the Modern Age only grows more  and more urgent as opportunities to  interface with and disappear into  technology become increasingly prevalent  likewise in Scorn the playable character  themselves is changed over the course of  the game at various points in the early  chapters the player can spot a strange  creature scuttling like a lizard along  the walls called the parasite this life  form eventually merges with the  protagonist providing them with useful  abilities but slowly taking over their  body to complicate this theme the  parasite is themselves a warped humanoid  who after being exposed to mysterious  chemicals has lost their identity with  various subtle hints indicating they're  the humanoid the player takes control of  during the game's prologue While most of  scorn explores how Machinery can  assimilate the organic the parasite  storyline seems to explore autonomy from  a different perspective asking where one  identity ends and another begins  near the assembly lies a gaping Abyss  known as the crater a forgotten void the  depths of the crater are home to form  more dangerous forms of life presiding  over this nightmarish domain is a  gargantuan nameless Monarch larger than  a building the creature acts as the  insect Queen to the life that infects  the crater constantly producing new  forms of workers these smaller more  animalistic Terrors Patrol the corridors  surrounding the crater and take on a  variety of shambling forms according to  game director ludimir peklar these  beings are made from the same genetic  material but undergo rapid evolutionary  changes resulting in distorted  monstrosities with occasionally  recognizable animal features in some  areas flying creatures have evolved  flitting about the corners of the  player's Vision menacing you from a  distance all these creatures serve the  Monarch at the craters Center and will  rush to their defense if they are harmed  and most every member of The Brood is  dangerous with the largest creatures  worth avoiding at all costs like snails  leaving a trail of slime the creatures  of the crater leave behind twisting  masses of red tendrils these tentacles  undulate slowly and seem to be turning  the atmosphere of the crater into  something more comfortable for The Brood  in some places this malignant infection  has become as dense as a rainforest as  if an entirely new ecosystem is emerging  in these cavernous depths  in the distance a vast rail line  promises a path away from this vile land  if you reach the station the tracks run  for many miles but eventually the  railway takes you to scorn's Final  Destination the empty city of polis at a  glance polis seems entirely unlike other  regions with clean walls and elegant  sculptural architecture yet the more of  this abandoned city the player explores  the more you notice the darkness that  dwells under the surface  inside the central building impossibly  ornate murals depict unsettling scenes  with reverent detail these Twisted  tableaus show humanoids being turned  into something else the citizens of  polis seem to worship Transcendence from  a corporeal form with the destruction of  Self in ever-present theme in this  regard Scorn evokes the work of Giger a  final time as he was known for making  similar murals in the epicenter of the  room a bronze automaton beckons to the  sky above the statue's head the vaulted  ceiling is covered in an immense network  of neuron-like strings of tissue given  all we have seen it appears likely that  the vanished citizens of polis live  within this network having abandoned  their physical bodies in favor of a  mental Collective what it is like to be  part of this Collective is an open  question for those with within no longer  have the means to answer  yet this city is not completely empty  locked away in jars or the pale  diminutive homunculi artificial beings  who appear to serve as living batteries  like many life forms in scorn these  creatures were created as a living  resource their only individuality  determined by the shape of their jars  yet homunculi are quite intelligent able  to create an interface with cybernetic  bodies to overcome their physical  confines while in game you don't  encounter these creatures much outside  the rare boss fight concept art shows  the resourcefulness depicting them  assembling cyborgs like car mechanics in  a garage  although not cited as a direct influence  many aspects of scorn's World building  evoke the Demonic realm depicted in  Barlow's Inferno an art book by the  legendary Wayne Barlow Barlow's Inferno  envisions an infernal afterlife where  the souls and bodies of inhabitants are  a resource to be used by diabolical  ruling class while visually the two  works or in many ways distinct the  worlds possess intriguing thematic  parallels whether or not the nightmarish  realm of scorn is a literal hell is  largely left up to interpretation but if  a land is so Bleak that it's impossible  to distinguish from a plane of eternal  torment there's an argument to be made  that on some level the distinction  ceases to matter  but perhaps there is salvation in the  world of scorn in the back of the  central building behind a far more  biological looking mural a door opens up  to a long walkway at the end of this  path is a swirling Matrix of energy that  seems to offer a form of transcendence  before the threshold of the Nexus  statues of humanoids melt into dreamlike  strands indicating what will happen if  you step inside perhaps entering this  Matrix is a way to join the collective  the citizens of polis seem to have  joined or perhaps it is something else  entirely in any case it's implied that  converging with this Nexus whatever it  truly is would mean complete  Annihilation the total loss of oneself  is this really salvation or just another  form of Destruction I certainly won't  give away the ending to scorn in this  video but suffice to say the game never  turns its back on the exceedingly Bleak  atmosphere it's so carefully crafted  throughout its storyline  the world that scorn builds is a grim  disturbing land of ceaseless torment  there's a lot of unsettling imagery in  this game much more than I've shown on  screen here so be aware of that if you  plan to check it out for yourself yet  the world of scorn for all its alien  qualities is undeniably familiar from  the factories to the barren environments  to the droves of people losing  themselves to the Bittersweet Oblivion  of Technology the game is full of  connections to the current state of our  species and piecing together subtle  World building details and interviews  with the game's creators it seems that  scorn is indeed supposed to be a future  version of Earth where the catastrophic  misuse of technology has left our  species unrecognizable ultimately scorn  represents a worst case scenario for the  story of humanity  with all the potential this world has I  hope the developers continue it in some  form and show us more of their  biomechanical vision  [Music]  as always thanks for watching  if you enjoyed this entry please lend  your support by liking subscribing and  hitting the notification icon to stay up  to date on all things curious see you in  the next video  foreign

